	<!-- ══ A1 · the front page ══ -->
	<article class="sheet land" id="front">
		<header class="flag">
			<h1 class="nameplate">The Utku Times</h1>
			<p class="motto" aria-label="Unpredictable, unsatisfied, unconventional.">
				<span class="word"><button class="un" type="button" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true" data-w="predictable">Un</button><span class="rest">predictable,</span></span>
				<span class="word"><button class="un" type="button" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true" data-w="satisfied">un</button><span class="rest">satisfied,</span></span>
				<span class="word"><button class="un" type="button" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true" data-w="conventional">un</button><span class="rest">conventional.</span></span>
			</p>
			<div class="folio-bar"><span>Vol. <span id="vol">{edition.vol}</span> · No. <span id="no">{edition.no}</span></span><span id="date">{dateline}</span><span class="opt">Ankara · CV edition</span></div>
		</header>

		<div class="cols">
			<div class="main">
				<p class="kicker">Profile · Ankara</p>
				<h2 class="lead-h">Developing story: Mehmet Utku Öztürk</h2>
				<p class="deck">Programming since eight, working on machine learning since thirteen, and nowhere near finished. The machine learning developer and AI researcher works on artificial consciousness and &ldquo;thinking&rdquo; systems. This story will be updated.</p>
				<figure class="photo">
					<div class="plate"><img src={`${A}/desk.webp`} width="1024" height="682" alt="Ink illustration of a cluttered desk with two laptops, and Mehmet Utku Öztürk asleep on the floor beneath it" fetchpriority="high" /></div>
					<figcaption>The desk in Ankara, and the developer under it. <span class="credit">Illustration: utku.space</span></figcaption>
				</figure>
				<div class="lead-body">
					<p>Mehmet Utku Öztürk wrote his first programs at eight: small apps and scripts. By eleven he was familiar with C++ and C#. At thirteen he started on machine learning, and at fourteen he fine-tuned his first large language model, &ldquo;extremely unsuccessfully,&rdquo; in his own words.</p>
					<p>The results have improved. In 2026 he co-founded <span class="nb">turkhukuk.ai</span>, a legaltech platform for Turkish lawyers, as its technical co-founder; its R&amp;D was accepted as a project at ODTÜ Teknokent. The same year he published HukukBERT as first author in the Journal of Computational Law and Legal Technology: the most comprehensive legal language model for Turkish.</p>
					<p>His experiments wander further out. FlyDOOM feeds the game Doom through the visual pathway of a real fruit fly connectome, 139,255 reconstructed neurons and roughly 2.7 million synapses, and maps the fly&rsquo;s motor output back to the controls. Nothing is trained; the wiring is the fly&rsquo;s. In LLM Electrodes he took the neuroscientist&rsquo;s electrode to a language model, and found that stimulating a single neuron is enough to change who it says it is.</p>
					<p>Before that he co-founded Clade AI, a personal assistant built on a self-deciding multi-agent framework, and pitched a $21 million round. He now works on LabGPT, a TÜBİTAK-backed tool that analyses test results for doctors, and mentors primary schoolers at BİLSEM, the government-led program for gifted children where he is a senior student.</p>
					<p>Away from the keyboard he is a competitive Class II powerlifter, also into strongman, Brazilian jiu-jitsu and kickboxing, and interested in cognitive science, kinesiology, chess, sudoku and good coffee. He is a member of Mensa International and the International Association for Artificial Intelligence and Law.</p>
				</div>
				<p class="src">The record continues on <a href="#record">A2</a>; research, interests, articles and the classifieds on <a href="#research">A3</a>.</p>

				<div class="secondary">
					<section class="story">
						<p class="kicker">Interpretability · AI</p>
						<h3>One neuron is enough to give a language model a new personality</h3>
						<p>Öztürk pushed each of the 294,912 MLP neurons of Qwen3.5-4B in both directions and had the strongest judged blind, with random neurons as controls. 130 passed every check. Asked the meaning of life, one replies: &ldquo;The meaning of life is $19.99, but it is now $14.99.&rdquo; No system prompt, no fine-tuning, no change to the weights.</p>
						<p><a class="chip" href="https://github.com/mutkuoz/llm-electrodes/blob/master/paper/main.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Paper ↗</a> <a class="chip" href="https://github.com/mutkuoz/llm-electrodes" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Code ↗</a> <a class="chip" href="https://doi.org/10.57967/hf/11353" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Data ↗</a></p>
					</section>
					<section class="story">
						<p class="kicker">Research · Law</p>
						<h3>HukukBERT beats BERTurk-Legal on a new legal cloze test</h3>
						<p>Trained on an 18 GB cleaned legal corpus with a 48K WordPiece tokenizer and domain-adaptive pre-training, the model reaches 84.40% top-1 accuracy on a novel Legal Cloze Test benchmark. Written with Tansu Türkoğlu and Buse Buz-Yalug.</p>
						<p><a class="chip" href="https://doi.org/10.47852/bonviewJCLLT620210346" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JCLLT ↗</a> <a class="chip" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.04790" target="_blank" rel="noopener">arXiv ↗</a></p>
					</section>
									</div>
			</div>

